%section.hero
  .container
    %div
      %p.eyebrow Passes ruby/spec
      %h1.hero-title
        :plain
          <span class="accent">Ruby 4.x</span>,<br>written in <span class="accent-2">Rust</span>.
      :plain
        <p class="hero-expansion"><b>Met</b>a <b>O</b>bject <b>R</b>untime <b>Ex</b>ecution</p>
      %p.hero-lede
        Metorex runs Ruby 4.x programs, and it exposes a method's syntax tree as objects your program can read at runtime.
      .hero-actions
        %a.button.button-primary{href: '/install'}
          Install
          :plain
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        %a.button{href: '/language'} Language tour
    .code-window
      .code-window-bar
        %span.dot.dot-1
        %span.dot.dot-2
        %span.dot.dot-3
        example.rb
      :plain
        <pre><code class="language-metorex">Point = Struct.new(:x, :y) do
          def +(other) = Point.new(x + other.x, y + other.y)
        end

        points = [Point.new(1, 2), Point.new(3, 4)]
        total = points.sum(Point.new(0, 0))
        puts total.inspect

        case total
        in { x: Integer =&gt; x, y: } if y &gt; x
          puts "y leads by #{y - x}"
        end</code></pre>
      .code-window-bar terminal
      :plain
        <pre class="sample-output"><code><span class="prompt">$ </span>metorex example.rb
        #&lt;struct Point x=4, y=6&gt;
        y leads by 2</code></pre>

%section.section
  .container
    %p.eyebrow Features
    %h2.section-title What Metorex does today
    %p.section-lede
      Each of these works in the current build.
    .features
      .feature-card
        %span.feature-icon{'aria-hidden' => 'true'} 4.x
        %h3 Ruby 4.x syntax
        :plain
          <p>Metorex reads Ruby 4.x source with its own lexer and parser. <code>RUBY_VERSION</code> reports the MRI release it follows, now <code>4.0.6</code>.</p>
        %a.feature-link{href: '/language'} Language tour →
      .feature-card
        %span.feature-icon{'aria-hidden' => 'true'} { }
        %h3 Code as objects
        %p A method's body is available at runtime as syntax tree nodes you can inspect.
        %a.feature-link{href: '/docs/meta'} Read a method's tree →
      .feature-card
        %span.feature-icon{'aria-hidden' => 'true'} ✓
        %h3 Built-in test runner
        :plain
          <p><code>metorex --test DIR</code> finds and runs <code>*_test.rb</code>, <code>test_*.rb</code>, and <code>*_spec.rb</code> files.</p>
        %a.feature-link{href: '/docs/rex'} Rex →
      .feature-card
        %span.feature-icon{'aria-hidden' => 'true'} .c
        %h3 C extensions
        %p Loads C extensions written against Ruby's C API.
        %a.feature-link{href: '/docs/c-extensions'} C API →
      .feature-card
        %span.feature-icon{'aria-hidden' => 'true'} &gt;&gt;
        %h3 REPL
        :plain
          <p><code>metorex --repl</code> starts an interactive session.</p>
        %a.feature-link{href: '/docs/cli'} Command line →
      .feature-card
        %span.feature-icon{'aria-hidden' => 'true'} rs
        %h3 Written in Rust
        %p The lexer, parser, and interpreter are written in Rust.
        %a.feature-link{href: '/architecture'} Architecture →

%section.section
  .container
    .notice
      %div
        %h2 Metorex passes the ruby/spec suite.
        :plain
          <p>Every spec in core, library, language, command_line, optional, and security runs in CI. If a program behaves differently under Metorex than under MRI, open an issue with the program and both outputs.</p>
      .hero-actions
        %a.button.button-primary{href: 'https://github.com/metorex-lang/metorex/issues/new', target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener'} Open an issue
        %a.button{href: 'https://github.com/metorex-lang/metorex/pulls', target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener'} Pull requests
